  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Comparison operators >=, <=, and <> compare kind-ordered values without coercion under en-ZA collation",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0.20527.20088, >=, <=, and <> share Excel's non-coercive comparison rules: numbers, text, and logicals compare in distinct ordered kinds (so =A1<>2 returns TRUE when cell A1 holds text \"2\"); errors propagate left-to-right without numeric coercion (#N/A<>\"a\" and \"a\"<>#N/A both return #N/A, while #DIV/0!<=#N/A returns #DIV/0!); and text compares case-insensitively under en-ZA collation (\"a\"<=\"A\" and \"B\">=\"b\" are TRUE, \"æ\"<=\"ae\" is TRUE, and \"é\"<>\"e\" is TRUE).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured across Excel 16.0.20527.20088 in cfunc_m1_2 (mixed_operands rows 61, 76-78; error_order rows 31-35, 41-45, 51-55) and cdoc_jpha (text_and_number_comparison rows 7, 940, 941, 943).",
